"Johann Sebastian Bach (1685–1750) was a German composer and musician of the late Baroque period. He was born in Eisenach, the youngest child of a city musician, into a family that produced three to four generations of musicians. He was orphaned at the age of 10. He learned the violin, and he was also an organist. Our Lesson 13 shows what Baroque music is like.": "c12c4968d6f4",
"Johann Sebastian Bach (1685–1750) was a German composer and musician of the late Baroque period. He was born in Eisenach, the youngest child of a city musician, into a family that produced three to four generations of musicians. He was orphaned at the age of 10. He learned the violin, and he was also an organist. Our Lesson 13 shows what Baroque music is like. In 1703 Bach became the organist at the New Church. He returned to Weimar as court organist in 1708, and he became a director of music in 1717. In 1723 he was appointed Thomaskantor, the director of church music in Leipzig, and he held that position for 27 years, until his death. He directed the St. Thomas School and provided four churches with music. A cantata was required for the church services on each Sunday, and his body of work includes more than 200 cantatas. Bach’s music encompasses every genre of his time aside from opera. He wrote extensively for the organ and other keyboard instruments, and he also wrote orchestral and chamber music. He is known for his mastery of counterpoint. Many of his works use techniques like canon and fugue, which he did not invent but contributed to so fundamentally as to influence many followers. A canon is a technique that employs a melody with one or more imitations of the melody. The first melody is the leader, and the imitating melody, in a different voice, is the follower. In the Taiwan dictionary, one voice comes first, and the other voices join with the same melody in turn, chasing one another to the last bar. A round is a limited type of canon: many voices sing exactly the same melody, each beginning at a different time. A voice here means a single melodic line. Counterpoint is the relationship of two or more simultaneous musical lines. The lines depend on each other harmonically, yet they are independent in rhythm and melodic contour. A fugue is a technique in two or more voices built on a subject, which then sounds successively in each voice. The word comes from the Latin fuga, “flight”, and from the verbs for “to flee” and “to chase”. A fugue is closer to a style of composition than to a fixed form, so in this lesson we only play with canons. Why is Bach called the father of music? In Taiwan’s dictionary he is “a founder of modern Western music” with the title “father of music”. Chinese Wikipedia says this label came into Chinese from Japanese scholars, has its limits, and is not yet widely accepted in the field. We could not find the title in English sources. What the sources do say is clear: after he died his reputation as a composer declined for a while, and he was remembered as an organist and a teacher. Haydn, Mozart, and Beethoven knew his music, and Felix Mendelssohn’s 1829 performance of the St. Matthew Passion precipitated the Bach Revival.": "83ee9e3dd29c",
